{"page": 14, "text": "(1) For the twelve months ended September 30, 2026, restructuring, asset-related and other charges primarily includes employee separation charges related to the 2026 Restructuring Program as well as charges related to our decision to exit our SPS CapstoneTM business. For the twelve months ended September 30, 2025, restructuring, asset-related and other charges primarily include charges related to our decision to exit our SPS CapstoneTM business and the 2024 Restructuring Program. See \"Note 4 \u2013Restructuring, Asset-Related and Other Charges\" that will be included in the Interim Consolidated Financial Statements in our Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2026 for further details. (2) For the twelve months ended September 30, 2026, loss on extinguishments of debt reflects costs associated with early redemption of the 2027 senior unsecured notes and partial early redemptions of our 2028 tranche B-3 euro term loan during the second and third quarter of 2026. See \"Note 15 - Debt\" that will be included in the Interim Consolidated Financial Statements in our Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2026 for further details. (3) For the twelve months ended September 30, 2026, gain on sales of assets and businesses, net includes a gain on sale of $316 million, related to parcels of land at the Company's former manufacturing site in Kuan Yin, Taiwan. See \"Note 10 - Property, Plant, and Equipment, Net\u201d that will be included in the Interim Consolidated Financial Statements in our Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2026 for further details. (4) For the twelve months ended September 30, 2026, transaction costs include $4 million of costs associated with the Senior Secured Credit Facilities. See \"Note 15 - Debt\" that will be included in the Interim Consolidated Financial Statements in our Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2026 for further details. (5) Qualified spend recovery represents costs and expenses that were previously excluded from Adjusted EBITDA, reimbursable by DuPont and/or Corteva as part of our cost-sharing agreement under the terms of the MOU which will be discussed in further detail in \"Note 17 \u2013 Commitments and Contingent Liabilities\" to the Interim Consolidated Financial Statements in our Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2026. (6) Litigation-related (income) charges, net, pertain to certain litigation settlements and reserves established, PFOA drinking water treatment accruals, and other related legal fees. For the twelve months ended September 30, 2026 and September 30, 2025, litigation-related charges includes $110 million and $287 million related to PFOA and PFAS matters, respectively. See \"Note 17 \u2013 Commitments and Contingent Liabilities\" that will be included in the Interim Consolidated Financial Statements in our Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2026 for\n"}
{"page": 15, "text": "further details. (7) Environmental charges pertain to management\u2019s assessment of estimated liabilities associated with certain remediation expenses at various sites. For the twelve months ended September 30, 2026, environmental charges primarily include changes to remediation reserves related to the preliminary Consent Decree entered into with the Environmental Protection Agency, (\"EPA\"), as well as the West Virginia Department of Environmental Protection (\"WVDEP\") during 2026. For the twelve months ended September 30, 2025, environmental charges primarily include changes to remediation reserves at the four sites covered by the New Jersey settlement agreement and off-site remediation costs at Dordrecht Works. See \"Note 17 \u2013 Commitments and Contingent Liabilities\" that will be included in the Interim Consolidated Financial Statements in our Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2026 for further details. (8) Adjustments made to income taxes relate to the removal of certain discrete income tax impacts within our provision for income taxes to enhance comparability between periods. This includes $208 million and $153 million related to the recognition of valuation allowances against certain U.S. federal and state deferred tax assets for the twelve months ended September 30, 2026, and September 30, 2025, respectively. During the second quarter of 2026, the Company concluded that a full valuation allowance was required on its U.S. federal and state net deferred tax assets. This conclusion was driven by multiple environmental and legal reserves recorded and the impact of these charges on the Company's long-term forecast of U.S. taxable income used in determining the realizability of U.S. deferred tax assets. This included significant legal and environmental reserves related to the announced settlement with the EPA and WVDEP as well as litigation matters in North Carolina. During the second quarter of 2025, the Company had established a partial valuation allowance on certain of its U.S. federal and state net deferred tax assets, primarily related to 163(j) interest carryforward deferred tax assets. This was driven by the environmental and legal reserves recorded related to the announced settlement with the State of New Jersey. Due to the long period by which these legal and environmental matters settle, which are for 15 years or longer, the related pre-tax charges affect projected future U.S. earnings and management's assessment of the realizability of certain deferred tax assets over a significant period. These valuation allowance adjustments are attributable to discrete legal and environmental events and the related reassessment of deferred tax asset realizability, rather than the Company's ongoing core operating performance. Because these valuation allowance adjustments significantly impact current reported income tax expense and reduce comparability across reporting periods, management believes excluding these impacts\n"}
{"page": 16, "text": "provides a more meaningful comparison of underlying operating performance between periods. While future valuation allowance adjustments may occur as facts and circumstances change, the adjustments recognized in each period reflect specific assessments based on the conditions existing at that time. See \"Note 6 \u2013 Income Taxes\" that will be included in the Interim Consolidated Financial Statements in our Quarterly Report on Form 10-Q for the quarter ended September 30, 2026, for further details. (9) The income tax impacts included in this caption are determined using the applicable rates in the taxing jurisdictions in which income or expense occurred for each of the reconciling items and represent both current and deferred income tax expense or benefit based on the nature of the non-GAAP financial measure. (10) For the twelve months ended September 30, 2025, accelerated depreciation charges of $23 million, incurred as part of our decision to exit our SPS CapstoneTM business are included within the \"Restructuring, asset-related and other charges\" caption above, and therefore are not included as separate adjustment within this caption. (11) Net Leverage Ratio calculated using GAAP measures is defined as our total debt principal, net, or our total debt principal outstanding less unrestricted cash and cash equivalents, divided by income (loss) before income taxes. Net Leverage Ratio calculated using non-GAAP measures is defined as our total debt principal, net, or our total debt principal outstanding less unrestricted cash and cash equivalents, divided by Adjusted EBITDA.\nThe Chemours Company\nReconciliation of GAAP Financial Measures to Non-GAAP Financial Measures (Unaudited)\n(Dollars in millions, except per share amounts)\nGAAP Earnings per Share to Adjusted Earnings per Share Reconciliation (1) (2)\nAdjusted earnings per share (\u201cAdjusted EPS\u201d) is calculated by dividing Adjusted Net Income by the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding. Diluted Adjusted EPS accounts for the dilutive impact of stock-based compensation awards, which include unvested restricted shares. Diluted Adjusted EPS considers the impact of potentially-dilutive securities, except in periods in which there is a loss because the inclusion of the potentially-dilutive securities would have an anti-dilutive effect.\nThree Months Ended\nNine Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\nJune 30,\nSeptember 30,\n2026\n2025\n2026\n2026\n2025\nNumerator:\nNet Income (Loss) attributable to Chemours\n$\n196\n$\n46\n$\n(274)\n$\n(107)\n$\n(339)\nAdjusted Net Income\n31\n26\n64\n102\n137\nDenominator:\nWeighted-average number of common shares outstanding - basic\n151,330,846\n150,320,265\n151,225,044\n151,109,721\n150,160,586\nDilutive effect of the Company's employee compensation plans (1)\n578,749\n461,349\n1,588,513\n995,663\n406,871\nWeighted-average number of common shares outstanding - diluted (1)\n151,909,595\n150,781,614\n152,813,557\n152,105,384\n150,567,457\nBasic earnings (loss) per share of common stock (2)\n$\n1.29\n$\n0.31\n$\n(1.81)\n$\n"}
{"page": 17, "text": "(0.71)\n$\n(2.26)\nDiluted earnings (loss) per share of common stock (1) (2)\n1.29\n0.31\n(1.81)\n(0.71)\n(2.26)\nAdjusted basic earnings per share of common stock (2)\n0.21\n0.17\n0.42\n0.68\n0.91\nAdjusted diluted earnings per share of common stock (1) (2)\n0.21\n0.17\n0.42\n0.67\n0.91\n(1) In periods where the Company incurs a net loss, the impact of potentially dilutive securities is excluded from the calculation of EPS under U.S. GAAP, as their inclusion would have an anti-dilutive effect. As such, with respect to the U.S. GAAP measure of diluted EPS, the impact of potentially dilutive securities is excluded from our calculation for the nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, and the three months ended June 30, 2026. With respect to the non-GAAP measure of adjusted diluted EPS, the impact of potentially dilutive securities is included in our calculation for the three and nine months ended September 30, 2026 and 2025, and the three months ended June 30, 2026 as Adjusted Net Income was in a net income position. (2) Figures may not recalculate exactly due to rounding. Basic and diluted earnings (loss) per share are calculated based on unrounded numbers.\nThe Chemours Company\nReconciliation of GAAP Financial Measures to Non-GAAP Financial Measures (Unaudited)\n(Dollars in millions, except per share amounts)\nGAAP Cash Flow Provided by Operating Activities to Free Cash Flows and Free Cash Flow Conversion Reconciliation (1)\nFree Cash Flows is defined as cash flows provided by (used for) operating activities, less purchases of property, plant and equipment as shown in the consolidated statements of cash flows. Free Cash Flow Conversion is calculated as the percentage of Free Cash Flows to Adjusted EBITDA.\nThree Months Ended\nNine Months Ended\nSeptember 30,\nJune 30,\nSeptember 30,\n2026\n2025\n2026\n2026\n2025\nCash flows provided by (used for) operating activities\n$\n149\n$\n146\n$\n158\n$\n263\n$\n127\nLess: Purchases of property, p